# One line ending in the repository, whatever the tool that wrote the file used.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS: without it, git stores whatever bytes it is handed, and
# the big files here ended up mixed — client.html once had 2544 lines but only
# 379 CR bytes. Any editor that normalises a file on save then rewrites every
# line of it, and git reports the whole file as changed. That is not cosmetic:
# merging five commits in October 2026 produced sixteen conflicting hunks in
# client.html, one of them 322 lines against 327, for what was a thirty-line
# change. Hand-merging a phantom conflict that size is how a real bug gets in.
#
# `text=auto` makes git store LF for anything it detects as text, converting on
# the way in. A working copy with CRLF therefore shows no diff at all, so the
# problem cannot come back no matter what writes the file. The working copy
# itself is left alone — no `eol=` here — so Windows tools keep the CRLF they
# already have and the Linux quality-gate workflow keeps LF.
* text=auto

# Windows-only, and PowerShell is the one thing here that genuinely prefers CRLF.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Declared ahead of time: no binary asset is tracked today, and a future one
# must never be line-ending converted.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.mp4 binary
